  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?",
  "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y=0 \\\\ -2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-x+2y=0\\,\u21d2\\,2y=x\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{x}{2}.$",
